1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,600
Hi, my name is Lauren, you might know me as Potato on X, and I work on GrokBot at SpaceX AI.

2
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:12,440
So last month, I did something pretty crazy.

3
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:17,240
I shipped 2,000 pull requests to production.

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:22,040
A lot of how I'm able to do this is through trust.

5
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:32,000
And I think a lot about trust in terms of how I can trust my agents to produce high quality work even when I'm not there.

6
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:42,260
And my argument and thesis for this talk today is that if you set up your environment for your agents really, really well,

7
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:48,320
you can end up with something that looks more like a personal or even team software factory,

8
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:55,600
where you're producing very high quality code at much greater rates than before.

9
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,740
But I'm not a fan of the term software factory.

10
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:06,440
I like the analogy of a Michelin kitchen better, where, you know, as technologists,

11
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:11,920
we're not really producing, we're not mass producing a product on an assembly line.

12
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,840
But the work that we do looks very creative.

13
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:18,300
It's very, you know, it's the act of building a product.

14
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:21,200
And it's art in some sense.

15
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:30,140
So, you know, even though with agents, we're not cooking the individual components that go into the product anymore,

16
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:37,960
we're still responsible for the final outcome and thinking about our kitchen setup.

17
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:38,400
Right.

18
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:39,300
Right.

19
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:45,600
Because depending on how you set up your line cooks, your sous chefs, you know, the kind of equipment they have,

20
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:52,600
the kind of training they have, you know, dishwashers and the ratio, I guess, of line cooks to dishwashers,

21
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
all of these ingredients go into making the final product.

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,220
And I think this analogy is really apt.

23
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,980
So I wanted to talk a little bit about a story before we begin, where six months ago,

24
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:18,180
when I first joined Cursor, before we were a part of SpaceX AI, I obviously had, you know,

25
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:20,940
no agent skills to use.

26
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:21,580
Right.

27
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:22,560
I just joined the company.

28
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,940
It was a fresh code base and a fresh product that I was working on.

29
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:37,180
So at the time, Cursor was building the replacement for the Cursor IDE, which is the new agents window.

30
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:43,980
And before I joined, the Cursor agent window had quite a lot of performance issues.

31
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:48,540
And my manager at the time asked if I was able to help them.

32
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:56,200
And since I had spent some time on the React team before I joined Cursor, it seemed like a good fit.

33
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:03,160
But when I first started, I quickly realized how manual this process was.

34
00:03:04,040 --> 00:03:06,240
Obviously, I have done performance work before.

35
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:17,880
But at the rate at which pull requests were being landed, it was just, it felt almost an insurmountable wall of pull requests that just kept coming in.

36
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,120
And I had no idea whether or not the performance of the app would be regressing.

37
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:23,320
Right.

38
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:36,800
So a lot of my early time on the Cursor team was spent looking at Chrome dev tools and the performance and doing performance traces and taking heap snapshots.

39
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:40,640
And it was extremely, extremely manual.

40
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:50,880
It was so manual, it got to the point where I just got really frustrated and started to think about, you know, wait, we have agents.

41
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:51,660
What am I doing?

42
00:03:51,660 --> 00:04:05,280
And so I started thinking about verification skills, where, you know, what if my agent could actually run the application itself and take the traces for me, understand traces and find the hotspots.

43
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:12,600
And basically, he'll climb, you know, to better performance in our application automatically.

44
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:23,460
And throughout the last six months of being at Cursor and SpaceX AI, you can really see that my productivity has skyrocketed.

45
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:29,520
And, you know, I never set out to ship 2,000 pull requests a month.

46
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,440
That was not a goal of mine at all.

47
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:41,840
But I realized that all of the skills, all of the tools and code-based changes I were making laddered up to this idea of trust.

48
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:50,400
And, you know, I didn't know it at the time, but I had this thought in the back of my head, which was, you know, I am the bottleneck.

49
00:04:50,800 --> 00:05:04,160
And I need to be able to take all of the knowledge that I have as an engineer and impart them into my team of agents so that I didn't need to be the blocker for everything.

50
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:09,520
And you can clearly see that it's paid off.

51
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:16,980
So, I think it really comes down to trust.

52
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:20,380
But how exactly do you build that trust?

53
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:27,020
And where do you start if you are, you know, wherever you are in your journey of using agents?

54
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:41,720
So, when I started, obviously, I was in this category, you know, the 1 to 1 to 5 range, where you still feel like you have to babysit every chat and conversation.

55
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:50,420
And you're just constantly course correcting, you know, you're intervening, you're correcting your agents so that it does the right thing.

56
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:57,960
And if you're not there, basically, nothing happens and the agents do the wrong thing.

57
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:05,180
And I would actually argue that this part, this phase of, you know, using agents is actually the hardest to get out of.

58
00:06:05,180 --> 00:06:10,020
Because it's not always very clear how exactly you get out of it.

59
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:13,860
And again, it just comes down to trust.

60
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:26,320
You know, the reason you're unable to go from 1 or 1 to 5 agents to something more, like 100, is because you don't have trust in your agents' work yet.

61
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:42,060
So, if you don't have trust and you try to spawn 100 sub-agents or cloud agents, you're going to quickly find that you're just going to get a ton of slot pull requests and, you know, a bunch of regressions and a bunch of bugs shipped and no one's going to be very happy with them.

62
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,200
So, that begs the question, how do you trust your agents more?

63
00:06:49,200 --> 00:07:04,780
So, for me, it came, it really started when I, like I said, I joined the cursor team and I was starting to work on performance, where I realized the need for verification.

64
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:10,200
So, when I say verification, there are sort of levels to that.

65
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:24,440
Because, you know, on the, I guess, the lower end of the scale for verification, you have things like the verification skills that I talk about, where you teach your agent how to run your application.

66
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:42,880
You know, and use things like the Chrome DevTools protocol or whatever other protocol that you have for debugging and teach them how to, you know, debug the application, take performance traces, take heat snapshots and so on.

67
00:07:42,880 --> 00:08:12,400
And then on the opposite end of the spectrum, which is much, much harder and still very much an open question, is like more formal verification, where, you know, maybe you rely on formal methods or, you know, languages like Lean or TLA Plus to do, you know, verification so that you can check that, you know, your business level or business logic invariants are,

68
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:23,780
you know, are always true and that you can formally verify that your application is always in a correct state.

69
00:08:24,780 --> 00:08:36,740
But I would say that, you know, even if you don't have the ability to run formal methods, and very few people really do, that with verification skills, you can get very far.

70
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:54,960
So when I joined Cursor and started to work on the Cursor Agent window, the first skill that I built was the skill called Control Glass, which is a verification skill that teaches the agent how to run the application and take traces, like I mentioned.

71
00:08:54,960 --> 00:08:59,140
And it does that through the Chrome DevTool protocol.

72
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:09,060
Something interesting about that skill is, and I kind of iterated my way to this, it didn't start out this way.

73
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:17,300
But the control, the control or verification skill really has two components to it.

74
