76
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:47,280
So what's really important is this idea of a feature map. So a feature map really is something that I kind of coined, I guess, where, you know, we started using these control skills with the cursor, then we quickly realized that, you know, a slack report would come in and a user would post a very vague screenshot, right, like a very small slice of the UI, and just like three question marks, and the agents using our control skills,

77
00:10:47,300 --> 00:11:17,300
are just no idea, like it could run the application, but it was just guessing at what exactly the user meant. And so I had this idea to create something called a feature map, which is, I guess, kind of inspired by like a site map. It essentially is a form of materialized memory, you know, like how exactly does your application work? What features does it have? How does a user reach it? You know, in terms of keyboard short

78
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:47,300
cuts, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know,

79
00:11:47,300 --> 00:12:17,300
like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are, you know, like, what elements are

80
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:32,180
but the ability for an agent to verify its own work is extremely powerful and we have spent a lot of time on this skill and it's very, very powerful for building that trust.

81
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:42,000
In addition to verification, of course, you want, because verification is really about correctness.

82
00:12:42,820 --> 00:12:55,640
Correctness to me is really about does the thing, does the feature or code do thing that you want it to do, right, like, does the, you know, the checkout button, does it actually check out the card?

83
00:12:55,640 --> 00:13:03,000
Verification is really important for that because you can get empirical evidence that this feature actually works.

84
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:10,440
But it doesn't tell you much about, you know, the performance or the code quality of that feature.

85
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:17,160
And that's where you start thinking about skills that teach agents to work like real software engineers.

86
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:23,160
So I've built a plugin called Pstack.

87
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:34,000
I'm not going to talk about the plugin too much today, but a lot of inspiration for that plugin, which is a collection of skills that I've created,

88
00:13:34,840 --> 00:13:46,280
are inspired by the kind of workflows that I personally have used in my time doing software engineering for all sorts of different types of tasks.

89
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:51,560
So debugging, you know, feature development, prototyping.

90
00:13:52,120 --> 00:14:01,220
There's a whole bunch of different playbooks and skills that ship in Pstack that teach your agents how to write code the way that you want them to.

91
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:16,020
And, you know, this is where, you know, the more experienced engineers on your team can really contribute to set up a team repository of skills that just make your agents a lot smarter.

92
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:31,960
And when you combine those skills with verification skills, then you're able to get to a point where your agents are able to not just verify that the work that they're doing is correct, but that it's also high quality.

93
00:14:31,960 --> 00:14:47,380
And again, because of verification, you can collect real performance metrics, you can get real numbers and statistics and telemetry on the performance of the application.

94
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:53,380
So I think that's a really important part to invest in.

95
00:14:55,720 --> 00:15:02,740
Another thing I think is really important is refactoring and rewriting your architecture to be more agent friendly.

96
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:12,340
And I almost want to say that this is one of the most important things you can do as a software engineering team,

97
00:15:12,340 --> 00:15:18,700
because if you really truly believe that agents are going to be writing all the code in the future,

98
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:24,720
then we need to design our code bases so that they do the right thing by default.

99
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:32,620
And you'll find that there's this, I almost want to say like a scale or continuum between,

100
00:15:32,620 --> 00:15:37,460
you know, these different pieces of building trust in your agents,

101
00:15:38,420 --> 00:15:42,120
where on, you know, I have like five of these points here.

102
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:48,880
Where the code base is really like the best form of memory,

103
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:53,840
because agents love to extend existing patterns that they see.

104
00:15:55,220 --> 00:15:59,840
And, you know, I think this is just the nature of how LLMs work,

105
00:16:00,060 --> 00:16:07,740
where they're more likely to use whatever it is in their context window to make changes.

106
00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:14,280
And of course, the files that the agents read and opens are part of its context window.

107
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:18,280
And therefore, the code base is a really important part of that.

108
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:24,280
Because, you know, agents aren't going to just refactor your code in every single PR.

109
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:27,280
They're going to just look at what's already there and just extend.

110
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:33,820
The next level, I think, is about static analysis, where you have linters,

111
00:16:33,820 --> 00:16:38,780
you have compiler diagnostics, you have continuous integration.

112
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:46,320
And these are guidelines and constraints that you can enforce in your code base,

113
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:52,740
so that whenever you correct your agent, you find that, you know, they just keep making the same mistake.

114
00:16:52,740 --> 00:16:59,320
You can add those as lint rules, or even better, you can refactor your code base,

115
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:04,320
so that the mistake that the agent is making becomes categorically impossible.

116
00:17:05,460 --> 00:17:10,260
And then a step above that, right, where, and this is where we're starting to get more into

117
00:17:10,260 --> 00:17:17,020
less of like a hard constraint and enforcement and more into the realm of guidance.

118
00:17:17,580 --> 00:17:21,440
You have things like rules, you have bug bot, you have skills,

119
00:17:22,180 --> 00:17:28,660
which, you know, your agents will obviously sometimes and mostly use when they're doing their work,

120
00:17:28,660 --> 00:17:32,700
but there's also a chance that it might, for various reasons, you know,

121
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:39,440
forget to read a rule, or maybe the user that is piloting the agent ignores them.

122
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:45,000
So these aren't quite as enforceable, but also an important part of

123
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:51,080
setting up your environment so that you can really trust what your agents are doing.

124
00:17:52,940 --> 00:17:55,000
And then finally, you have a style guide,

125
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:59,000
which is really only enforceable by humans in a code review.

126
00:17:59,420 --> 00:18:03,620
I guess you could put these in your rules and bug bot and skills as well.

127
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:10,580
But if you don't, then you have this big glaring hole in your review process,

128
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:15,880
where now humans have to, you know, look at every single line that's being changed

129
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:17,140
and remember to comment.

130
00:18:17,140 --> 00:18:22,280
And with the rate of pull requests that are coming in, it just becomes impossible.

131
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:26,820
So I definitely wouldn't recommend, you know, relying only on the style guide.

132
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:32,460
I think the style guide or, you know, like looking at human reviews is a good place to start

133
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:35,180
in terms of what's missing.

134
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:40,620
But you should really invest the time to think about the other four parts.

135
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:48,560
You know, your code base, making things categorically impossible through better data structures or algorithms,

136
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:54,960
static analysis, and then of course you layer that with rules and bug bot and skills.

137
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:58,780
On the code base front,

138
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:03,520
and in the GrokBot code base,

139
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:09,520
we actually have invested into setting something up that we call Dune,

140
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:12,080
which is our agent-friendly framework.

141
00:19:13,140 --> 00:19:18,580
So the inspiration for Dune really came about from a lot of performance issues

142
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:22,400
we were seeing in the cursor agent window.

143
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:26,400
And so a lot of lessons came out of that exploration.

144
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:34,300
But the key principle that we landed on is really that agents love taking shortcuts.

145
00:19:35,720 --> 00:19:40,860
So what if we designed a framework such that the shortcut, you know,

146
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:44,360
the easy path is the right path for agents.

147
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:48,880
And also one that would be a code base that is, you know,

148
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:55,660
maybe pretty annoying for humans to work in because it's so locked down in terms of what you can do and what you can't do.

149
00:19:56,240 --> 00:20:00,020
But it actually creates the perfect environment for agents,

150
00:20:00,400 --> 00:20:04,060
especially ones that have very minimal context because, you know,

151
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:07,500
not every contributor to your code base is going to be an engineer anymore.

152
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:13,200
You can have designers, you can have product managers, you can have CEOs, you know,

153
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:15,760
going into the code base and shipping features.

154
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:21,440
So we want to really think a lot about how we invest and set up our code bases

155
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:26,640
so that, you know, even agents that are piloted by busy people

156
00:20:26,640 --> 00:20:30,060
with not a lot of context can do a good job by default.

157
00:20:30,060 --> 00:20:38,460
And like I mentioned before, your code base is really a form of memory for agents

158
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:42,340
because they love to extend the existing patterns that they see.

159
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:46,020
And the reverse is actually also true, right?

160
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:51,380
You can invest the time to set up your code base in a way that things are,

161
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:53,820
you know, bad patterns are categorically impossible,

162
00:20:53,820 --> 00:20:56,960
or you have like lit rules that prevent them.

163
00:20:56,960 --> 00:21:03,640
But the reverse is also true in the sense that if you have existing anti-patterns,

164
00:21:03,940 --> 00:21:08,060
you will actually find that these will spread kind of like a virus

165
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:15,540
where you have like one small workaround or a comment that explains a workaround.

166
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:19,300
And you'll quickly find that agents just love to copy that.

167
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:23,120
And then in a matter of a few days or a few weeks,

168
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:25,960
you'll find that that workaround has spread everywhere

169
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:31,920
and it's now becomes, it has become like a de facto pattern for all agents.

170
00:21:32,140 --> 00:21:34,400
And that's a really, really bad place to be in.

171
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:38,280
And that goes back to what I was saying about why it's really important,

172
00:21:38,780 --> 00:21:41,220
you know, whenever you're correcting your agents,

173
00:21:41,440 --> 00:21:45,540
that you invest the time into thinking about code base changes

174
00:21:45,540 --> 00:21:48,640
and static analysis to, and of course,

175
00:21:48,640 --> 00:21:53,720
layering them with rules, good rules and bug bot and skills

176
00:21:53,720 --> 00:21:56,900
because of that reason.

177
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:03,280
And another analogy that I like in addition to the Michelin kitchen

178
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:06,760
is this idea that, you know, your code base is kind of like a garden

179
00:22:06,760 --> 00:22:11,460
where you have workarounds, you know,

180
00:22:11,460 --> 00:22:15,200
that seem seemingly, that seem kind of innocent at first,

181
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:17,500
but then because of the nature of agents,

182
00:22:17,640 --> 00:22:19,660
you just copy that pattern over and over again

183
00:22:19,660 --> 00:22:25,300
and you quickly end up with a very, you know, vibe coded code base

184
00:22:25,300 --> 00:22:29,560
that is, you know, a pain in the butt to maintain

185
00:22:29,560 --> 00:22:31,820
and has a lot of performance issues.

186
00:22:31,820 --> 00:22:37,720
So in my opinion, the perfect agent code base is one that's so locked down

187
00:22:37,720 --> 00:22:42,400
that, you know, again, it's like really annoying for humans to write code in,

188
00:22:42,820 --> 00:22:46,260
but it's so conventional, it's so standardized

189
00:22:46,260 --> 00:22:51,380
that, you know, even innocent looking patterns are just forbidden.

190
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:54,640
And the best example of this I have

191
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:59,260
is actually something that seems very, very innocent when you look at it,

192
00:22:59,260 --> 00:23:02,080
but when you think about it, it's actually really bad.

193
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:07,580
And that pattern is agents leaving comments in the code.

194
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:11,760
Now, you know, when I first saw agents starting to do this,

195
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:16,940
I initially wasn't, well, I did think that a lot of them were sloth,

196
00:23:17,060 --> 00:23:19,480
but I also thought that, you know, it actually doesn't,

197
00:23:19,580 --> 00:23:21,240
it's not a bad thing, right, I guess,

198
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:23,640
if agents are leaving comments in the code

199
00:23:23,640 --> 00:23:27,060
because as humans, we left comments in the code

200
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:29,400
whenever we saw, you know, edge cases

201
00:23:29,400 --> 00:23:32,660
or we needed to actually make a workaround

202
00:23:32,660 --> 00:23:35,320
or, you know, leave a note to ourselves

203
00:23:35,320 --> 00:23:39,660
or a colleague on a particularly tricky part of the code base.

204
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:43,320
But what I quickly realized

205
00:23:43,320 --> 00:23:46,520
when we saw this happening in the cursor code base

206
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:50,040
was that agents were just using the comments

207
00:23:50,040 --> 00:23:52,740
around the code as justification

208
00:23:52,740 --> 00:23:56,500
for why it wasn't going to solve the actual problem

209
00:23:56,500 --> 00:24:00,160
and instead paper over it with a band-aid

210
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:02,740
or a short-term solution.

211
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:08,540
So in Dune, which is, again, the framework that powers Grokbot,

212
00:24:09,300 --> 00:24:11,220
we made the choice to,

213
00:24:11,220 --> 00:24:16,000
we made the choice to actually ban comments

214
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,200
for that reason where,

215
00:24:18,440 --> 00:24:21,520
so that agents would not just copy that pattern

216
00:24:21,520 --> 00:24:26,120
and, you know, propagate it everywhere in the code base.

217
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:34,060
So my pitch here is that every team really needs something that,

218
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:36,640
you know, a role that I'm calling a gardener

219
00:24:36,640 --> 00:24:41,260
in the same way that, you know, with a real garden,

220
00:24:41,260 --> 00:24:44,900
then you need someone who is thinking a lot about the,

221
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:48,220
you know, things that can kind of creep in

222
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:50,920
and grow in ways that you don't want.

223
00:24:51,040 --> 00:24:52,340
Like, you know, you have weeds,

224
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:57,360
you have, you know, just other types of organic growth.

225
00:24:57,840 --> 00:24:59,480
I don't really know gardening that well.

226
00:25:00,680 --> 00:25:03,240
But you have things that, you know, unwanted pests

227
00:25:03,240 --> 00:25:06,560
and stuff like that that kind of creep in empirical base.

228
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:09,200
And so you want to nip them in the bud

229
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:13,160
as soon as possible before they start propagating everywhere.

230
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:17,400
A lot of the principles behind Dune

231
00:25:17,400 --> 00:25:19,420
are really centered around these three things.

232
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:22,700
First of all, we want to delete tech depth

233
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:24,600
that we already have for, you know,

234
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:25,940
for reasons I just mentioned.

235
00:25:26,620 --> 00:25:31,940
We want to keep or enforce a single paved path

236
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:34,000
for most blessed patterns.

237
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:37,040
You know, there should be one conventional way

238
00:25:37,040 --> 00:25:40,640
to do some things so that agents don't really need to guess.

239
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:42,720
And there should be enough guidance

240
00:25:42,720 --> 00:25:47,240
in the code base, in CI, in lint rules,

241
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:51,440
so that the agents are guided to follow that path.

242
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:57,380
And then finally, whenever you see tech depth or bad patterns,

243
00:25:57,940 --> 00:25:59,060
your instinct should be,

244
00:25:59,260 --> 00:26:01,220
I need to write a lint rule against it.

245
00:26:01,560 --> 00:26:03,520
You don't always have to clean it up immediately

246
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:06,020
because if you write a lint rule,

247
00:26:06,100 --> 00:26:07,460
you can at least stop the bleeding

248
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:11,100
and which, you know, doesn't solve the problem entirely,

249
00:26:11,300 --> 00:26:13,080
but it at least prevents it from growing.

250
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:15,660
So I definitely recommend, you know,

251
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:16,720
really thinking a lot about

252
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:21,040
how you can guard against anti-patterns

253
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:24,140
so that they don't spread like a virus.

254
00:26:24,140 --> 00:26:26,880
And then also spend time to actually,

255
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:29,520
you know, get your agents to clean them up

256
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:32,240
so that your code base is just constantly kept

257
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:36,000
in a state where you would be happy

258
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:37,560
if an agent were to copy it.

259
00:26:38,060 --> 00:26:40,920
That's the kind of mindset that I would recommend having.

260
00:26:42,360 --> 00:26:44,040
And then I won't actually go through

261
00:26:44,040 --> 00:26:45,480
all the details of Dune itself,

262
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:48,620
but I'll just kind of gloss through some interesting parts.

263
00:26:49,460 --> 00:26:50,800
So again, as a reminder,

264
00:26:51,020 --> 00:26:52,080
Dune is the architecture,

265
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:55,180
the client framework that we built to power Grokbot.

266
00:26:56,760 --> 00:26:58,220
We've invested a lot into,

267
00:26:58,400 --> 00:26:59,480
you know, all the things I was saying,

268
00:26:59,640 --> 00:27:01,880
where we have conventions.

269
00:27:02,080 --> 00:27:04,500
We have a lot of conventions about where code should live

270
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:10,880
and where and how code should be imported between them.

271
00:27:11,240 --> 00:27:12,840
So in Dune applications,

272
00:27:13,240 --> 00:27:15,140
you know, there's different concepts

273
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:16,520
where, like, for example,

274
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:18,960
features are all co-located in a single folder.

275
00:27:19,780 --> 00:27:21,260
You have an entry point.

276
00:27:21,260 --> 00:27:24,880
You have a lot of strict boundaries between these things,

277
00:27:24,880 --> 00:27:25,500
that determines,

278
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:27,440
you can kind of think of it like a route.

279
00:27:28,500 --> 00:27:29,900
You have transcript cards

280
00:27:29,900 --> 00:27:31,760
that show up in the Grokbot application.

281
00:27:32,560 --> 00:27:34,720
You have a host that runs on the,

282
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:36,920
you know, the Grokbot virtual machine.

283
00:27:37,540 --> 00:27:39,240
And then, of course, you have your client,

284
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:43,620
which powers the overall Dune application.

285
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:50,000
And we have a lot of strict boundaries between these things,

286
00:27:50,240 --> 00:27:53,860
where, just as an example,

287
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:56,380
things that run on the main process,

288
00:27:57,120 --> 00:27:58,840
or the main thread in Electron,

289
00:27:59,240 --> 00:28:01,400
aren't allowed to be run on the renderer thread.

290
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:03,980
And we keep that separation very intentionally,

291
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:08,480
because of lessons we learned from Cursor's agent window,

292
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:14,600
where we would sometimes see code accidentally get imported

293
00:28:14,600 --> 00:28:15,840
into the renderer thread,

294
00:28:16,440 --> 00:28:17,580
and, you know, slow code.

295
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:21,380
And since on the renderer thread,

296
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:26,200
you want your UI to be very smooth and performant,

297
00:28:26,200 --> 00:28:29,360
you need to make sure that you don't have any long tasks,

298
00:28:29,640 --> 00:28:33,280
or, you know, things that take longer than 16 milliseconds,

299
00:28:33,720 --> 00:28:35,980
or if you want, like, 60 frames per second,

300
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:37,520
or 8 milliseconds,

301
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:39,800
if you want 120 frames per second.

302
00:28:40,820 --> 00:28:44,920
And so your renderer has to be constantly in a state

303
00:28:44,920 --> 00:28:46,680
where it is,

304
00:28:47,240 --> 00:28:49,020
it can really kind of chunk up the work

305
00:28:49,020 --> 00:28:51,340
and not do them all at once.

306
00:28:51,340 --> 00:28:55,880
And so we have code within Dune

307
00:28:55,880 --> 00:28:58,660
that enforces these boundaries

308
00:28:58,660 --> 00:29:01,660
through the import and dependency graph.

309
00:29:02,460 --> 00:29:05,120
But, yeah, that's just an example of a pattern

310
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,480
that we saw lead to really bad performance

311
00:29:07,480 --> 00:29:11,340
that we categorically eliminated

312
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:15,740
through the architecture of Dune.

313
00:29:17,900 --> 00:29:20,260
And then all of these other pieces

314
00:29:20,260 --> 00:29:21,420
aren't that interesting.

315
00:29:22,920 --> 00:29:26,540
But, again, the core theme here,

316
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:27,900
you know, it's not about Dune,

317
00:29:28,380 --> 00:29:29,780
but the idea that

318
00:29:29,780 --> 00:29:34,640
an agent-friendly framework of your own

319
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:35,980
is actually very, very powerful,

320
00:29:36,260 --> 00:29:38,520
and it can encode all of the learnings

321
00:29:38,520 --> 00:29:41,060
that you and your, you know,

322
00:29:41,120 --> 00:29:42,540
your best engineers on your team

323
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:44,940
have tribal knowledge of.

324
00:29:45,660 --> 00:29:48,560
And I think the lesson here is that

325
00:29:48,560 --> 00:29:49,960
how do you take that away

326
00:29:49,960 --> 00:29:51,280
from, you know,

327
00:29:51,340 --> 00:29:54,220
what used to be in the style guide process

328
00:29:54,220 --> 00:29:55,920
of reviewing code

329
00:29:55,920 --> 00:29:56,880
and, you know,

330
00:29:57,340 --> 00:30:00,100
engineers reviewing other engineers' work

331
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:01,440
and leaving comments

332
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:04,280
to extract,

333
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:05,940
almost like extracting that knowledge

334
00:30:05,940 --> 00:30:08,040
and encoding that into the framework,

335
00:30:08,580 --> 00:30:10,060
into the code base itself,

336
00:30:10,260 --> 00:30:13,220
so that the code base accesses the memory, right?

337
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:14,940
It's the thing,

338
00:30:15,500 --> 00:30:16,520
I keep coming back to this idea

339
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:17,140
that, you know,

340
00:30:17,180 --> 00:30:18,980
the code base is just the thing

341
00:30:18,980 --> 00:30:21,920
that it's the materialized snapshot

342
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:23,180
of the state

343
00:30:23,180 --> 00:30:25,780
in which you want your agents to extend,

344
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:27,560
and you want that code base

345
00:30:27,560 --> 00:30:28,620
to be so pristine,

346
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:29,640
so great,

347
00:30:29,780 --> 00:30:31,720
that the next agent that comes along

348
00:30:31,720 --> 00:30:32,620
is just very likely

349
00:30:32,620 --> 00:30:33,940
to continue that pattern

350
00:30:33,940 --> 00:30:36,200
and keep it really, really good.

351
00:30:36,200 --> 00:30:39,900
And if you spend enough time

352
00:30:39,900 --> 00:30:41,060
on this process,

353
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:42,760
like I mentioned,

354
00:30:42,860 --> 00:30:44,400
you can really set up

355
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:46,180
a Michelin kitchen

356
00:30:46,180 --> 00:30:47,340
or a software factory

357
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:48,720
where,

358
00:30:49,300 --> 00:30:52,220
because you've spent so much time

359
00:30:52,220 --> 00:30:54,320
on, you know,

360
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:55,380
all of these pieces

361
00:30:55,380 --> 00:30:56,820
that allow you to trust your agents,

362
00:30:57,460 --> 00:30:59,160
whether it's in the code base,

363
00:30:59,680 --> 00:31:01,120
whether it's lint rules,

364
00:31:01,120 --> 00:31:03,360
whether it is,

365
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:05,000
you know,

366
00:31:05,060 --> 00:31:06,640
diagnostics or rules

367
00:31:06,640 --> 00:31:08,240
or bugbot or skills,

368
00:31:08,580 --> 00:31:11,420
these layers come together

369
00:31:11,420 --> 00:31:14,160
and provide you a lot of trust,

370
00:31:14,500 --> 00:31:15,200
because now,

371
00:31:15,700 --> 00:31:15,960
you know,

372
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:17,040
just imagine for a moment

373
00:31:17,040 --> 00:31:19,040
you're working in the bugbot code base.

374
00:31:20,940 --> 00:31:22,280
It's super locked down,

375
00:31:22,460 --> 00:31:22,640
you know,

376
00:31:22,680 --> 00:31:24,420
it's like almost impossible

377
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:25,460
to write bad code,

378
00:31:25,840 --> 00:31:26,720
so, you know,

379
00:31:26,760 --> 00:31:27,060
you can,

380
00:31:27,260 --> 00:31:27,800
even an agent

381
00:31:27,800 --> 00:31:29,080
with very little context,

382
00:31:29,780 --> 00:31:30,620
you know,

383
00:31:30,620 --> 00:31:31,580
even an agent

384
00:31:31,580 --> 00:31:32,740
with not a lot of reasoning

385
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:34,340
can come in

386
00:31:34,340 --> 00:31:35,720
and actually write code

387
00:31:35,720 --> 00:31:36,320
that's good.

388
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:39,000
And going back to my example

389
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:40,140
about the Michelin factory,

390
00:31:40,300 --> 00:31:40,900
I think there's

391
00:31:40,900 --> 00:31:42,260
a lot here,

392
00:31:42,380 --> 00:31:42,620
right,

393
00:31:42,700 --> 00:31:42,940
where,

394
00:31:43,400 --> 00:31:43,640
you know,

395
00:31:43,640 --> 00:31:44,920
we're setting up our agents,

396
00:31:45,100 --> 00:31:45,540
our bots,

397
00:31:45,640 --> 00:31:47,860
with skills and tools,

398
00:31:48,460 --> 00:31:48,680
you know,

399
00:31:48,700 --> 00:31:49,640
we're training them,

400
00:31:50,120 --> 00:31:51,380
we're setting up our kitchen

401
00:31:51,380 --> 00:31:53,040
in a way that makes sense,

402
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:53,380
right,

403
00:31:53,780 --> 00:31:56,420
for the agents and bots

404
00:31:56,420 --> 00:31:57,540
to do the right thing

405
00:31:57,540 --> 00:31:58,200
by default.

406
00:31:59,080 --> 00:31:59,560
You know,

407
00:31:59,560 --> 00:32:00,420
whenever we see,

408
00:32:00,660 --> 00:32:01,020
for example,

409
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:02,160
in a kitchen example,

410
00:32:02,160 --> 00:32:03,880
if we notice that

411
00:32:03,880 --> 00:32:05,300
one of our cooks

412
00:32:05,300 --> 00:32:06,380
or dishwashers

413
00:32:06,380 --> 00:32:07,400
is constantly tripping

414
00:32:07,400 --> 00:32:08,020
over something,

415
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:09,640
of course we need to fix that,

416
00:32:10,100 --> 00:32:10,300
right,

417
00:32:10,300 --> 00:32:11,300
we need to problem solve

418
00:32:11,300 --> 00:32:12,800
and ensure that,

419
00:32:12,840 --> 00:32:13,020
you know,

420
00:32:13,020 --> 00:32:14,200
others don't trip as well

421
00:32:14,200 --> 00:32:14,560
because,

422
00:32:14,780 --> 00:32:14,940
you know,

423
00:32:14,940 --> 00:32:15,360
in a kitchen

424
00:32:15,360 --> 00:32:16,580
it's a very dangerous place

425
00:32:16,580 --> 00:32:17,140
and you don't want

426
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:17,860
to hurt yourself.

427
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:21,240
It's the same mindset,

428
00:32:21,500 --> 00:32:21,760
I think,

429
00:32:21,820 --> 00:32:22,440
that we should have

430
00:32:22,440 --> 00:32:23,180
with our code bases.

431
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:25,800
How do we set it up

432
00:32:25,800 --> 00:32:27,020
so that even agents

433
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:30,460
without a lot of knowledge

434
00:32:30,460 --> 00:32:32,380
can do a good job?

435
00:32:33,860 --> 00:32:35,500
And I think with,

436
00:32:35,500 --> 00:32:36,680
you know,

437
00:32:36,760 --> 00:32:37,200
Grokbot,

438
00:32:37,900 --> 00:32:39,040
Grokbot and Cursor

439
00:32:39,040 --> 00:32:40,460
play an interesting role together

440
00:32:40,460 --> 00:32:43,760
where Grokbot is really great

441
00:32:43,760 --> 00:32:46,240
at providing what I call

442
00:32:46,240 --> 00:32:46,940
the outer loop

443
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:49,260
because you can connect Grokbot

444
00:32:49,260 --> 00:32:50,560
to lots of different,

445
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:51,900
you know,

446
00:32:51,960 --> 00:32:52,560
different connectors

447
00:32:52,560 --> 00:32:53,580
like Slack

448
00:32:53,580 --> 00:32:54,820
to Datadog,

449
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:55,380
Sentry,

450
00:32:56,320 --> 00:32:57,420
PlanetScale,

451
00:32:57,680 --> 00:32:58,460
whatever services

452
00:32:58,460 --> 00:32:59,320
that you use

453
00:32:59,320 --> 00:33:00,700
and you can aggregate

454
00:33:00,700 --> 00:33:01,580
all of that information

455
00:33:01,580 --> 00:33:02,020
together

456
